Since $\omega \in A^0$, Lemma (lem:certainty-knowledge of the paper) implies $m_A(\omega) \subseteq A^0$, as required.  The argument for $B^0$ is symmetric.

For any event $F$, the set $C_A(F)$ is $A$-saturated.  Indeed, if $\omega \in C_A(F)$, then:
\begin{equation}
p^A_{m_A(\omega)}(F) = 1,
\end{equation}
and certainty reflection (with $q_A = 1$) gives:
\begin{equation}
p^A_{m_A(\omega)}(C_A(F)) = 1.
\end{equation}
Since $\omega \in C_A(F)$, Lemma (lem:certainty-knowledge of the paper) implies $m_A(\omega) \subseteq C_A(F)$.

