{"artifact_digest":"sha256:e6f0d44d46c7bb85f4554a4a20caab4c0c0f74500ce3abfa298036d06fd5900d","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/e6/e6f0d44d46c7bb85f4554a4a20caab4c0c0f74500ce3abfa298036d06fd5900d.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:bdd6a2bac597ea5e0b028b12e9608fc8044e9625c314bee8f5b3784067b29f92","bundle_entry_key":"2608-12291-theorem-21-time-lip","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_094","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_094.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-12291-proposition-21-time-lip","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: We first take $T<\infty$. If $x\in\{0,1\}$, then the statement holds since $\tau^*_T(t,x)=0$ almost surely, so fix any $x\in(0,1)$. We will show the result through the Lagrange-formulated problem $W_T$ defined in \eqref{eq:lagrange_formulation}.
    We will first prove \eqref{eq:time_deriv_bound} by bounding the left and right derivatives from below and above, respectively. 

    Let $t\in(0,T)$ and $h>0$ such that $t-h\geq0$, and assume, for simplicity, that $h\leq1$. Denote the optimal stopping time by $\tau^*:=\tau^*_T(t,x)$. Then $\tau^*$ is admissible for the problem with initial data $(t-h,x)$. We have 
    \begin{equation*}
