body:
[Thm of arXiv:2506.18207, 'Cartan's Path Development, the Logarithmic Signature and a Conjecture of Lyons-Sidorova'] Under the same assumption of Theorem (windingangletheorem of the paper). If $\theta$ is piecewise $C^1$ on $[0,T]$ and $\theta_T-\theta_0\in(4k\pi+\pi,4k\pi+3\pi)$ for some integer $k$, then $\gamma$ has finite radius of convergence for its logarithmic signature.
\begin{proof}
It is implied by Corollary (ODEequivalence of the paper), Remark (piecewiseC2path of the paper) and the calculation in (equation signcalculation of the paper). Actually, we have 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
&\int_l^{l+\pi}\rho_T^\lambda\cos(\varphi_T^\lambda-\zeta)d\zeta\\
\to&~e^{\lambda T}\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}\cos\left(\frac{\theta_{T_k+}-\theta_{T_k-}}{2}\right)\int_l^{l+\pi}\cos\left(\zeta-\frac{\theta_0}{2}\right)\cos\left(\zeta-\frac{\theta_T}{2}\right)d\zeta\\
=&\frac{e^{\lambda T}\pi}{2}\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}\cos\left(\frac{\theta_{T_k+}-\theta_{T_k-}}{2}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\theta_T-\theta_0}{2}\right).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
W.L.O.G. we can assume the all the intersection angles $|\theta_{T_k+}-\theta_{T_k-}|<\pi$ for any $k$. Actually, we have to assume this to make the ODE system consistent on each piece $\gamma|_{[T_k,T_{k+1}]}$, otherwise the condition has to change. Because $\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}\cos\left(\frac{\theta_{T_k+}-\theta_{T_k-}}{2}\right)>0$, it follows that
$$
\int_l^{l+\pi}\rho_T^\lambda\cos(\varphi_T^\lambda-\zeta)d\zeta\to-\infty~~~\Longleftrightarrow~~~\cos\left(\frac{\theta_T-\theta_0}{2}\right)<0,
$$
which finishes the proof.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem L1 of arXiv:2609.01350, 'New classes of trace-form permutation polynomials and their compositional inverses'] Let $a, b \in \mathbb F_{2^n}$ , $b \neq 0$ and $f(x) = x^3 + ax + b, g(y) = y^2 + by + a^3$. Let $y_1$, $y_2$ be two roots of $g(y)$. The factorizations of $f(x)$ are characterized as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f = (1, 1, 1)$ if and only if ${\rm Tr}_2^{2^n} \left( \frac{a^3}{b^2} \right) = {\rm Tr}_{2}^{2^n}(1)$, and $y_1, y_2$ are cubes in $\mathbb F_{2^n}$ (resp. $\mathbb F_{2^{2n}}$) if $n$ is even (resp. odd);
\item $f = (1, 2)$ if and only if ${\rm Tr}_2^{2^n} \left( \frac{a^3}{b^2} \right) \neq {\rm Tr}_2^{2^n}(1)$;
\item $f = (3)$ if and only if ${\rm Tr}_2^{2^n} \left( \frac{a^3}{b^2} \right) = {\rm Tr}_2^{2^n}(1)$, and $y_1, y_2$ are not cubes in $\mathbb F_{2^n}$ (resp. $\mathbb F_{2^{2n}}$) if $n$ is even (resp. odd).
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition def:Bernoulli-split of arXiv:2607.14993, 'Sharp phase transition for percolation with short-range dependencies'] Let $\Omega = \{0, 1\}^{E \times \llbracket 1, M \rrbracket} \times \{0, 1\}^{\mathbb Z^d \times \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket}$. We define, for $e \in E$ and $l \in \llbracket 1, M \rrbracket$,
  \begin{equation}
    Y_{e, l}: (y, z) \in \Omega \mapsto y(e, l) \in \{0, 1\}
  \end{equation}
  and, for $x \in \mathbb Z^d$ and $k \in \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket$,
  \begin{equation}
    Z_{x, k}: (y, z) \in \Omega \mapsto z(x, k) \in \{0, 1\}.
  \end{equation}
  We call these Bernoulli variables base variables. We define for $l \in \llbracket 1, M \rrbracket$ the function
  \begin{equation}
    p_l: \beta \in \mathbb R_+^* \mapsto 1 - \frac {1 - h_l(\beta)} {1 - h_{l-1}(\beta)} \in [0, 1].
  \end{equation}
  Let $\mathbb P$ be the product probability measure on $\Omega$ such that
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb P(Y_{e, l} = 1) = p_l(\beta) \text{ for all $e \in E$,$l \in \llbracket 1, M \rrbracket$} 
  \end{equation}
  and
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb P(Z_{x, k} = 1) = \frac {q_\nu(\llbracket k, N \rrbracket)} {q_\nu(\llbracket k-1, N \rrbracket)}
    \text{ for all $x \in \mathbb Z^d$, $k \in \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket$.}
  \end{equation}
  We define
  \begin{equation}
    \nu_x = \min \{n \in \llbracket 0, N \rrbracket : \forall 1 \leqslant k \leqslant n, Z_{x, k} = 1 \}
  \end{equation}
  and we say that an edge $e = \{x, y\}$ is open if
  \begin{equation}
    \max\limits_{1 \leqslant l \leqslant \zeta(\nu_x, \nu_y)} Y_{e, l} = 1.
  \end{equation}

[Pro 12 of arXiv:2609.02879, 'On the cut locus of Hamilton--Jacobi equations I: structure and propagation via the touchi'] Let \( \Omega \subset \R^n \) be an open convex subset and $\phi\in\text{\rm SCL}\,(\Omega)$. Then
	\begin{align*}
		\reg(\phi)=\big\{x\in\Omega:\partial_P \phi(x)\neq\varnothing\big\},\qquad \cut(\phi)=\big\{x\in\Omega:\partial_P \phi(x)=\varnothing\big\}.
	\end{align*}
	Moreover, for any $x\in\reg(\phi)$, $\phi$ is differentiable at $x$ and $\partial_P \phi(x)=\{D\phi(x)\}$.

[Lemma lm:bulinsk of arXiv:2411.11429, 'Functional Central Limit Theorem for topological functionals of Gaussian critical points'] [Bulinskaya]

Let \( p'<p \) be integers, \( A \) a bounded Borel  set of \( \mathbb R^{p'} \). For $ 1\le i\le p,$ let \( g_i:A\to \mathbb R^{d_i} \) be a smooth centred Gaussian field  {such that\begin{itemize}
\item $ \|g_i(x)\|$ and $ \| \nabla g_i(x)\|$ are bounded  by $ \| (\partial _{ \alpha }F(x);\alpha \in I_i)\|$ for some finite $ I_i$,
\item   $G(x) := (g_i(x))\in \mathbb R^{p^{*}}
$ has a uniformly bounded density, where \( p^{*} = \sum_id_i.\)
\end{itemize} }
Let \( h_i:\mathbb R^{d_i}\to \mathbb R \)  smooth functions such that $ h_{ i}$ and its derivative are bounded by $c_{ l} \|x\|^{ l}$ for some $l>0$, and \( f_i(x): = h_i(g_i(x)),1\le i\le p. \) Assume also that each \( h_i(X) \) has a density bounded on \( [-\varepsilon, \varepsilon ] \) for some \( \varepsilon >0 \) on the input \( X\sim \mathcal N(0,I_{d_i}) \). Let
 \(a\in (0,1) \) and
\begin{align*}\psi (x) = (f_i(x))_{i = 1}^p\in \mathbb R^p.
\end{align*}  There is \( c_a ,\tau_a >0 \) such that for \( \tau \in (0,\tau_a ) \), 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf P(\inf_{x\in A}\|\psi (x)\|\le \tau )\le c_a  (1 +  \text{\rm{diam}}(A))^{2d} \tau ^{a }.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm:itolyons of arXiv:2606.28869, '\bfseries A General Theory of Paths:\\ \large Signatures, Jump Lifts, and Expected Signatu'] Fix $N<\infty$ and a bounded set of weakly geometric $p$-rough paths. The map
\begin{equation}
\mathbf x\longmapsto \mathrm{Sig}^{(N)}(\mathbf x)
\end{equation}
from the $p$-variation rough-path topology to $T^{(N)}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ is continuous.

[Lemma 7 (Local Asymptotic) of arXiv:2606.06489, 'The Missing Central Limit Theorems for Local Functionals of Berry's Random Wave Model'] For all $0<M<\infty$,
    \begin{equation*}
        \sup_{|a+b|\leq M/R} \left|  \frac1{R^3}K_R(a,b)-\pi^2F(R(a+b)) \right|=o(1),\quad R\to \infty.
    \end{equation*}

