{"artifact_digest":"sha256:05ac0ed620f3f5a2ba88f61eb9db3e6b5baf970f8695b1b8d96393d61ef6e2e3","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/05/05ac0ed620f3f5a2ba88f61eb9db3e6b5baf970f8695b1b8d96393d61ef6e2e3.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:a1349e360c4ca1d542d91b405aef5d193cd6c7e4a7628775b66d613a7fec01b5","bundle_entry_key":"2411-14766-lemma-10-lem3","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_108","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_108.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2411-14766-lem-10-lem3","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: First we have to ensure that $\mathcal{X}_{\rho_{N_n^{\mathcal{Z}}}}$ is not too large, that is to say smaller than $ n^{1/2(1-\alpha)^{-1}+\epsilon}$ for any positive $\epsilon$. This fact is actually a consequence of Proposition (prop1 of the paper),  Corollary (cor2 of the paper) and Lemma (lem1 of the paper). Let us give some details : by Corollary (cor2 of the paper) and Lemma (lem1 of the paper) 
\begin{align*}
    &\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{X}_{\rho^{\mathcal{Z}}_{N_n^{\mathcal{Z}}}} > n^{1/2(1-\alpha)^{-1}+\epsilon}) =\\ 
