{"artifact_digest":"sha256:f74e43a6ccc0be9a6730c3b8341a26d0a875a88336bbf72630eb132e6c8f22df","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/f7/f74e43a6ccc0be9a6730c3b8341a26d0a875a88336bbf72630eb132e6c8f22df.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:6dfc6007a8df781d09125f492dcccf14d7708dde09a4ec3265fdaa6178c78c83","bundle_entry_key":"diophantine_small_divisor_estimates_rotation","bundle_id":"population.pivot2-dynamics-b","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"Diophantine approximation, small divisors, circle rotations","draft":"claude-pivot2-dynamics-b/bundle_pivot2-dynamics-b.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:dynamics.circle:diophantine-small-divisor-estimates-for-circle-rotations","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"(i) For real t one has |sin(pi t)| >= 2 ||t||: by periodicity and symmetry it suffices to check t in [0, 1/2], where sin(pi t) is concave and hence lies above the chord joining (0,0) to (1/2,1), i.e. sin(pi t) >= 2 t. Therefore |e^{2 pi i q alpha} - 1| = 2 |sin(pi q alpha)| >= 4 ||q alpha||, and the Diophantine hypothesis finishes the bound. The upper bound comes from |e^{i theta} - 1| <= |theta| applied with theta = 2 pi (q alpha - nearest integer).

(ii) If u in L^2 solves the equation, comparing Fourier coefficients gives (e^{2 pi i q alpha} - 1) u-hat(q) = f-hat(q). Since alpha is irrational, e^{2 pi i q alpha} is not 1 for q nonzero, so u-hat(q) is determined for q nonzero and u-hat(0) is free: this is uniqueness up to a constant, and it also forces the mean-zero condition on f (take q = 0). For existence, define u-hat(q) by that formula. Because f is C^infinity, for every N there is c_N with |f-hat(q)| <= c_N |q|^{-N}; by (i), |u-hat(q)| <= (4C)^{-1} c_N |q|^{tau - N}. Taking N > tau + k + 2 makes sum |q|^k |u-hat(q)| finite for every k, so the Fourier series and all its termwise derivatives converge absolutely and uniformly, and u is C^infinity and solves the equation. The Sobolev estimate is the same computation with weights.

(iii) For fixed q the set of alpha in [0,1] with ||q alpha|| < C q^{-tau} is a union of q intervals of length 2 C q^{-tau - 1}, of total measure 2 C q^{-tau}. For tau > 1 the series over q converges, so by Borel-Cantelli almost every alpha lies in only finitely many of these sets; discarding those finitely many q by shrinking C (and using that ||q alpha|| > 0 for each fixed q, alpha irrational) gives a constant valid for all q. The tau = 1 characterization is the classical equivalence between ||q alpha|| >= C/q for all q and boundedness of the partial quotients, read off from the continued fraction bounds 1/(q_{n+1} + q_n) < ||q_n alpha|| < 1/q_{n+1} together with the best-approximation property. Dirichlet's theorem gives infinitely many q with ||q alpha|| < 1/q, which rules out tau < 1.","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:d37b643574863d0d87862b211345cdbd8fa1472dd80b325dff599f991ba15499","revision_digest":"sha256:701f743bb2241950d3a8f90b87a8fd689c27198731a6afd43cc9166321190ecf","source_id":"wikipedia:Diophantine_approximation","source_revision":"as-read-2026-09-12","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

