{"artifact_digest":"sha256:9531973172a29c32b1047fb076d24c15619c38ac7d6597d98be1fd010fc4df71","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/95/9531973172a29c32b1047fb076d24c15619c38ac7d6597d98be1fd010fc4df71.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:0a479ba078f1f380508b9ad7dc16714d877d06b7804b9ad6c1fa8363590e6e30","bundle_entry_key":"2607-18505-theorem-23-4","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_111","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_111.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-18505-theorem-23-4","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: For now, we treat the tail index $\alpha$ as arbitrary. Define the sets
        \begin{align*}
			C_1 = \{(x_1, x_2) \in \mathbb{R}^2: |x_1+x_2|>1 \}, \\
			C_1^+ = \{(x_1, x_2) \in \mathbb{R}^2: x_1+x_2>1 \}, \\
			C_1^- = \{(x_1, x_2) \in \mathbb{R}^2: x_1+x_2<-1 \}.
		\end{align*}
        Then,
		\begin{align*}
			||X_1+X_2||_b^\alpha &= \lim_{s\rightarrow \infty} b(s)P(|X_1+X_2|>s) \\
			&= \lim_{s\rightarrow \infty} b(s)P\bigg(\frac{1}{s}(X_1, X_2)\in C_1\bigg) \\
			&= \nu_{\bm{X}}(C_1) = \nu_{\bm{X}}(C_1^+) + \nu_{\bm{X}}(C_1^-).
		\end{align*} 

