body:
[Proposition prop:tail of arXiv:2607.19844, 'Binomial probabilities at a fixed distance from the mode:\\ size-biasing and the complete '] For $|t|<Np$,
	\begin{equation}
		\sum_{k\ge1}\frac{1}{N^{k}}\cdot\frac{(-1)^{k}t^{k}}{k\,p^{k}}
		=-\log\Bigl(1+\frac{t}{Np}\Bigr)=-\log\frac{m}{Np},
	\end{equation}
	the series being convergent.  In truncated form, uniformly for $p\in K$ and $t\in H$ and for
	every $M\ge1$,
	\begin{equation}
		\sum_{k=1}^{M}\frac{1}{N^{k}}\cdot\frac{(-1)^{k}t^{k}}{k\,p^{k}}
		=-\log\frac{m}{Np}+O\bigl(N^{-M-1}\bigr),
	\end{equation}
	with an implied constant depending only on $M$, $K$ and $H$.

