[Defi prok of arXiv:2603.21634, 'Individual-based stochastic model with unbounded growth, birth and death rates: a tightnes'] The Prokhorov distance is defined for every $\mu,\nu \in M_{1}(\mathbb{R}^{*}_{+})$ by
\begin{align*}
d_{P}^{1}(\mu,\nu) := \inf \{ \varepsilon >0, \forall A \in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^{*}_{+}), \hspace{0.1 cm}  \mu(A) \leq \nu(A^{\varepsilon}) + \varepsilon, \hspace{0.1 cm} \nu(A) \leq \mu(A^{\varepsilon}) + \varepsilon \},
\end{align*}
where $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^{*}_{+})$ is the usual Borel $\sigma$-algebra on $\mathbb{R}^{*}_{+}$, and $A^{\varepsilon} := \{ y \in \mathbb{R}^{*}_{+}, \inf_{x \in A} |x-y| < \varepsilon \}.$ For any positive function $w$, we extend this definition to the $w$-Prokhorov distance $d_{P}^{w}$, defined for every $\mu,\nu \in \mathcal{M}_{w}(\mathbb{R}^{*}_{+})$ by
\begin{align*}
d_{P}^{w}(\mu,\nu) := d_{P}^{1}(w*\mu,w*\nu),
\end{align*} 
where $w*\mu$ is the usual pushforward of $\mu$ by $w$ (\textit{i.e.} for every measurable function $f$, $\langle w*\mu, f \rangle := \langle \mu, w f \rangle$).

[Definition recovery of arXiv:2507.08757, 'Strong existence and uniqueness of the tilt-indexed Busemann process in the planar corner '] Given real weights $\omega=(\omega_x)_{x\in\mathbb{Z}^2}\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{Z}^2}$, a function $A:\mathbb{Z}^2\to\mathbb{R}$ is said to \emph{recover} the weights $\omega$ if it satisfies the following \emph{recovery property}:
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
A(x,x+e_1)\wedgeA(x,x+e_2)=\omega_x\quad\text{for all $x\in\mathbb{Z}^2$}.
\end{aligned}\qedhere\end{equation}

[Lem ;lpdej of arXiv:2604.12462, 'Stochastic analysis of Beckner's and related functional inequalities'] The function $J$ satisfies the equation 
\begin{align}
 \frac{\partial J}{\partial s}(s,x)x^{q-2}+\frac{1}{2}\frac{\partial ^{2}J}{\partial x^{2}}(s,x)
 =x^{p-2},\quad s,x>0,
\end{align}
with initial condition $J(0,x)=0$ for $x>0$.

