body:
[Proposition prop.dpi of arXiv:2605.14149, 'The mean-field limit of online stochastic vector balancing'] Fix $p > 4$ and $(t_0,m_0) \in [0,1) \times \mathcal{P}_p({\mathbb R})$. Suppose that $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{F}, \mathbb{P})$ is a filtered probability space hosting a Brownian motion $B$, and an $\mathcal{F}(t_0)$-measurable random variable $X_0$ with $X_0 \sim m_0$. Let $\alpha$ be an $\mathbb{F}$-progressively measurable process with
    \begin{align*}
        \|\alpha(t)\|_2 \leq c_0,\qquad \|\alpha(t)\|_{\infty} \leq C \qquad \text{ for a.e.\ $t\in[t_0,t_0+h]$},
    \end{align*}
    for $c_0=\sqrt{2/\pi}$ and some arbitrary constant $C<\infty$. Then, for any $h\in(0,1-t_0)$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{V}_p^+(t_0,m_0) \leq \mathcal{V}_p^+\Big( t_0 + h, \mathcal{L}\big( X(t_0 + h) \big)\Big),
    \end{align*}
    where $X$ is defined by
    \begin{align*}
        X(t) \coloneqq X_0 + \int_{t_0}^t \alpha(s)\, ds + B(t) - B(t_0), \quad t_0 \leq t \leq 1.
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:smoothcycle of arXiv:2608.25279, 'Provable Non-Acceleration of Standard Strang\\ Splittings of Kinetic Langevin Dynamics'] Assume the hypotheses of Lemma~(lem:interiorcycle of the paper), and let $m\ge3$ satisfy the strict cycling condition (equation eq:strictPK of the paper). Then there exist distinct points $x_0^\circ,\ldots,x_{m-1}^\circ\in\mathbb R^2$ on the unit circle, a potential $U\in\mathcal U_\kappa^2$, and constants $r_0>0$, $b_\ast<\infty$ such that, with indices modulo $m$,
\begin{equation}
 x_{j+1}^\circ=(1+\beta)x_j^\circ-\beta x_{j-1}^\circ-s\nabla U(x_j^\circ),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
 \nabla U(x_j^\circ+u)=\nabla U(x_j^\circ)+u,
 \qquad \lvert u\rvert\le r_0,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 \nabla U(x)=x+w(x),\qquad \sup_{x\in\mathbb R^2}\lvert w(x)\rvert\le b_\ast.
\end{equation}
Consequently, $\nabla^2U=\mathrm{Id}_2$ on each ball $B(x_j^\circ,r_0)$, and the Jacobian of the deterministic heavy-ball update
\begin{equation*}
 (x,y)\longmapsto
 \bigl((1+\beta)x-\beta y-s\nabla U(x),\;x\bigr)
\end{equation*}
at every cycle state $(x_j^\circ,x_{j-1}^\circ)$ is
\begin{equation}
 \mathsf A_1=
 \begin{pmatrix}(1+\beta-s)\mathrm{Id}_2&\;-\beta\mathrm{Id}_2\\
                 \mathrm{Id}_2&\;0\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Therefore, the Jacobian of the $m$-step update at each cycle state is $\mathsf M=\mathsf A_1^m$, and
\begin{equation*}
 \rho(\mathsf M)
 =\rho(\mathsf A_1)^m
 \le\rho_{\mathrm q}(s,\beta;\kappa)^m
 <1.
\end{equation*}

[Lem lemma_density_wall_influence_times of arXiv:2412.03370, 'TASEP with a general initial condition and a deterministically moving wall'] Suppose $x^f_{\alpha T}(T) \simeq \xi T$ and $f$ fulfils Assumption~(assumpt:2.1.1 of the paper) with parameters $\alpha_0 < \dots < \alpha_n$. Then, it holds
	\begin{equation} 
	\rho^f_r(\xi) = \sqrt{ \frac{\alpha}{\alpha_0}} \ \text{ and } \  \rho^f_l(\xi) \leq \sqrt{\frac{\alpha}{\alpha_n}}. 
	\end{equation}   
	In the definition of $\rho^f_r(\xi)$, the limit for $\eta \downarrow 0$ exists. If $x^f_{\alpha T}(T)$ is in the interior of the region affected by the wall and Assumption~(assumpt:2.1.1 of the paper) is fulfilled with $\varepsilon \leq \alpha_n$, then this is also the case for $\rho^f_l(\xi)$, and we have $ \rho^f_l(\xi) = \sqrt{\alpha \alpha_{n \vphantom{0}}^{-1}}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

Let f: {-1,1}^n -> {0,1} with x uniform on {-1,1}^n, Walsh coefficients fhat(S) = E[f(x) prod_{i in S} x_i], derivative D_i f(x) = (f(x with x_i = 1) - f(x with x_i = -1))/2, and q_i = P(f(x) != f(x with x_i flipped)) the probability that coordinate i is pivotal. f is unate if for each i, D_i f >= 0 everywhere or D_i f <= 0 everywhere. For every such f, fhat({i}) = E[D_i f] and E[(D_i f)^2] = q_i / 4. If f is unate, then q_i = 2|fhat({i})| for every i, so the total influence sum_i q_i equals 2 sum_i |fhat({i})|, and the sign of fhat({i}) gives the direction in which f is monotone in coordinate i (when q_i > 0).
f maps {-1,1}^n to {0,1}
x is uniform on {-1,1}^n

