      Then we have
    \begin{equation}

        \lim_{t \rightarrow \infty}d_{LP}(\mu_t, \mathcal{S}_\kappa) = 0,
    \end{equation}
    where $d_{LP}$ is the Levy-Prokhorov metric. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2506.10817, 'On the Weak Error for Local Stochastic Volatility Models'] Let $\lambda,\delta \in (0,1)$ and $\mu\in \mathcal{P}_2(\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R})$  induced by some random variables $(\xi,X)$ such that $\xi$ is bounded from above and below as in Assumption \ref{Aspt_new}. For $j\ge 0$ consider $\Psi_j$ with its dependence on $\delta$ and $\lambda=c_{min}^2\rhobar^2h$ as defined in \eqref{Eq:Psi_Def}. Then the map $y \mapsto \Psi_j^{-\frac12}(y,\mu)$ is Lipschitz and for all $\gamma \in (0,1)$
\begin{align*}
        \| \Psi_j^{-\frac12}\|_{Lip} \lesssim \gamma^{-\frac12} \lambda^{-\frac{1+\gamma}2} \delta^{-\gamma}.
    \end{align*}

