{"artifact_digest":"sha256:4d32c70f557a1e653a06e963172f6d7725ee9b5086cd902d424fef3254e087b0","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/4d/4d32c70f557a1e653a06e963172f6d7725ee9b5086cd902d424fef3254e087b0.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:bd8cb8e8ed7ed4eac301b0c6ae17e1c1f041cd49e1c9f21d8b734d34f3735a60","bundle_entry_key":"2609-01576-lemma-18-mixed-tail-pointwise-measure-c","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_060","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_060.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2609-01576-lemma-18-mixed-tail-pointwise-measure-c","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Fix $j\in\cbr{1,2}$ and write $p_j:=\frac{1}{\alpha_j}$.
Let $\widetilde\mu_{j,K}$ be the probability measure supplied by
Lemma (lem:mixed_tail_ambient_restriction of the paper). For $t\in K$ and
$n\ge0$, define
\begin{align*}
r_{j,n}(t)
:=
\min\cbr{
r\in\cbr{d_j(t,s):s\in K}:
\widetilde\mu_{j,K}(B_{d_{j,K}}(t,r))
\ge2^{-2^n}
}.
\end{align*}
The minimum exists because $K$ is finite and the largest
$d_{j,K}$-ball centered at $t$ has
$\widetilde\mu_{j,K}$-mass one. Since the mass threshold
$2^{-2^n}$ is nonincreasing in $n$, the sequence
