body:
[Defn real-tree_def of arXiv:2601.00119, 'The largest common subtree of two random trees'] A compact metric space $(T,d)$ is a \emph{real tree} if for all $x,y\in T$, it holds that:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(a)] There is a unique isometric embedding $g:[0,d(x,y)]\to T$ such that $g(0)=x$ and $g(d(x,y))=y$; then write $\llbracket x,y\rrbracket=g([0,d(x,y)])$ and call it the \emph{geodesic from $x$ to $y$}.
    \item[(b)] If a function $h:[0,1]\to T$ is continuous and injective, then $h([0,1])=\llbracket h(0),h(1)\rrbracket$.
\end{enumerate}

[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2608.21255, 'Laws of the iterated logarithm for random Dirichlet series with general weights'] Assume that $\eta$ has a standard normal distribution. Then
    \begin{equation}
        \limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{\log|\hat{Z}_n|}{\log\log n}=\frac{1}{2}\quad \text{{\rm a.s}}.
    \end{equation}

