[Proposition prop:var_lb_stat_rand_meas of arXiv:2411.08848, 'Stationary random measures: Covariance asymptotics, variance bounds and central limit theo'] Let ${\mathcal X}$ be a stationary random measure on $\mathbb{R}^d, d \geq 2$ whose truncated correlation measure ${\bf K}$ has finite total variation and $\widehat{{\bf K}}$ vanishes at $\infty$. Let ${\mathcal X}_L$ be the scaled random measure defined by ${\mathcal X}_L(A) := {\mathcal X}(LA)$,  $L \geq 1$.  Then,  there exists a constant $c >0$ (depending only on $d$) such that for any bounded set $W$ and for all $L \geq 1$,  

\begin{equation}

{\rm Var}\!\left({\mathcal X}_L(W)\right)  \geq c\lambda_D \min \{|W|L^d,  |W|^{\frac{d-1}{d}}L^{d-1}\},
\end{equation}

where $| \cdot |$ denotes the Lebesgue measure. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop: wm around S2:2 of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] For every $\gamma\in (1,2)$, there exists $\bar \varepsilon_\gamma >0$ such that for all $(\lambda,C^2) \in S^0$ with $\tfrac{p-1-\lambda}{p}-  C^2 \leq \bar \varepsilon_\gamma $ we have the estimate
  \begin{equation*}
   1 - A\left( \frac{p-1-\lambda}{p}-C^2 \right) - \left( \frac{p-1-\lambda}{p}-C^2 \right)^\gamma < w_m <    1 - A\left( \frac{p-1-\lambda}{p}-C^2 \right) + \left( \frac{p-1-\lambda}{p}-C^2 \right)^\gamma,
  \end{equation*}
  with $A= \frac{-4}{g''(1)} = 2\left[\frac{p+1}{p} - \frac{\lambda}{p(p-1)} + 3 C^2 \right]^{-1} \in [\frac{p}{2p-1},2]$.

