[Corollary C:non d main of arXiv:2507.01236, 'Disintegration theorem for multifunctions, \\ with applications to empirical Wasserstein d'] Let $\mathfrak{X} = [0, 1]^D$ be the unit cube in dimension~$D$, endowed with the Euclidean metric $d$ and its Borel $\sigma$-field.
Suppose that~$\mu$ is an absolutely continuous probability measure on~$\mathfrak{X}$ with density $f$ satisfying
\begin{equation}

c \leq f(x) \leq C\mbox{\rm \ for every $x \in \mathfrak{X}$},
\end{equation} 
where $0 < c \leq C < \infty$. Given $\alpha \in (0, \infty)$, let
\begin{equation}

r(n)\equiv r(n,D, c, \alpha) := c^{-1/D} D^{1/2} (4 \cdot 3^{D - 1})^{1/D} (2 + \alpha)^{1/(2D)} \left( \frac{\ln n}{n} \right)^{1/(2 D)}.
\end{equation}
Then there exists $n_0 \equiv n_0(D, c, C, \alpha)$ such that, for every $n \geq n_0$, the space $(\mathfrak{X},d,\mu)$ is $(n^{-\alpha},r(n))$-disintegrable. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem finite-catch-up of arXiv:2410.24170, 'Persistent hubs in CMJ branching processes with independent increments and preferential at'] Suppose that Equation~(equation eq:persistent of the paper) is satisfied. Then, in the process $(\mathscr{T}_{t})_{t \geq 0}$, the set 
    \begin{equation} 
    \mathcal{P} := \left\{u \in \mathcal{U}\colon u \text{ catches up to each of its ancestors}\right\}
    \end{equation}
    is finite almost surely.

