[Lemma 25 (Monomial elimination of the earlier-vanishing system) of arXiv:2609.02449, 'Finite Directional Coefficient Modules over Euler Fibers of $A$-Hypergeometric Systems'] The map $\mu_{\Lambda}$ in \eqref{eq:classwise-monomial-elimination-map} satisfies
\begin{equation}

\Ker(\mu_{\Lambda})=U_{\Lambda}^{\mathrm{tr}}.
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}

\mu_{\Lambda}\left(U_{\Lambda,\bm{w}}^{<K}\right)=\mathfrak{Q}_{\Lambda,\bm{w}}^{<K}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}

\mu_{\Lambda}^{-1}\left(\mathfrak{Q}_{\Lambda,\bm{w}}^{<K}\right)=U_{\Lambda,\bm{w}}^{<K}.
\end{equation}

[Definition def:cQ-def of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] Given $\mathcal{S} = \mathcal{S}_\mathcal{X}$, the \emph{origami} function, denoted by $\mathcal{Q} = \mathcal{Q}_\mathcal{X} : \Lambda(G) \to \mathbb{R}$, is defined (up to a global additive constant) as a real-valued function. Its increments between two neighboring vertices $v^{\bullet}(c)$ and $v^\circ(c)$ are given by
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{Q}(v^\bullet(c))-\mathcal{Q}(v^\circ(c))\ :=\ |\mathcal{X}(c)|^2\,=\,|\mathcal{S}(v^\bullet(c))-\mathcal{S}(v^\circ(c))|\,.
\end{equation}
We will often shorten $|\mathcal{X}(c)|^2=\delta_c$ the length of the edge of $\Lambda(G)$ attached to the corner $c$. The alternate sum of edge-lengths in a tangential quad vanishes, which ensures the consistent definition for $\mathcal{Q}$. One can see $\mathcal{Q}$ as a folding of the quadrilaterals along their diagonals (see e.g. [CLR1]), which makes $\mathcal{Q}$ a $1$-Lipschitz in the $\mathcal{S} $ plane.

[Lemma lem:distinct-pair of arXiv:2609.02544, 'A finiteness theorem for geodesic Leech wheels'] If $L_w(\ell)\ge\tfrac12$, then
\begin{equation}
  s^{\times}(S)
  \le
  \frac{M_w+\binom{\ell}{2}}{2}
  -\frac{L_w(\ell)^2-L_w(\ell)}4.
\end{equation}

[Lemma Lem local asymp cut disks expect CR 1 8 of arXiv:2604.15146, 'Renormalised two-point functions of CLE$_4$ gaskets'] As $\max(\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2)\to 0$, we have that
\begin{multline*}
\mathbb{P}(E_{\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2}^{v,\rm{holes}})
