Let G be a finite connected graph with distinct edge weights, T its MST, and G_p the graph of edges with weight <=p. Kruskal's algorithm has, by level p, selected a spanning tree inside every component C of G_p. These selected edges remain in T. Hence the unique T path between any u,v in C lies inside C, and its length is at least their shortest-path distance in C. Consequently max_C diam_intrinsic(C)<=diam(T). Any probabilistic lower bound for the left side transfers directly to the MST diameter without a universality assumption.

The reverse inequality fails: on K_n give the edges of a Hamilton path the n-1 smallest weights and every other edge larger distinct weights. Kruskal selects exactly that path, so diam(T)=n-1. At a threshold including every edge, G_p=K_n has intrinsic diameter 1. Thus intrinsic cluster-diameter control at one level gives no MST upper bound. This does not refute high-dimensional scaling. The script checks all 38 connected labeled four-vertex graphs at every selected threshold and exact K_4,K_8,K_16 path examples. Full torus/hypercube bounds remain unresolved in this packet.","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:ded8fddb33a606a358455f809c33b51023b9de4319e7cfbdbc3fdfbe6e6a4a4b","revision_digest":"sha256:9ada7267569bf5e793c13e77b278a39da722c0b99f729a634167494629ef5b51","source_id":"owner-w344-open-problems-20261010:complete-graph-path-mst-reverses-no-diameter-upper-bound","source_revision":"2026-10-10 worker result; proof; independent audit pending w346; novelty unverified","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

