[Lemma Lemma 5.2 of arXiv:2504.06238, 'Infinite Boundary Friction Limit for Weak Solutions of the Stochastic Navier-Stokes Equati'] Let $\mathcal{H}_1, \mathcal{H}_2$ be Hilbert Spaces such that $\mathcal{H}_1$ is compactly embedded into $\mathcal{H}_2$, and for some fixed $T>0$ let $(\boldsymbol{\Psi}^n): \Omega \times [0,T] \rightarrow \mathcal{H}_1$ be a sequence of measurable processes such that \begin{equation}  \sup_{n\in \mathbb{N}}\mathbbm{E}\int_0^T\norm{\boldsymbol{\Psi}^n_s}^2_{\mathcal{H}_1}ds < \infty\end{equation} and for any $\varepsilon > 0$, 
    \begin{equation} \lim_{\delta \rightarrow 0^+}\sup_{n \in \mathbb{N}}\mathbbm{P}\left(\left\{\omega \in \Omega:\int_0^{T-\delta}\norm{\boldsymbol{\Psi}^n_{s + \delta}(\omega) - \boldsymbol{\Psi}^n_s(\omega)}^2_{\mathcal{H}_2}ds > \varepsilon\right\} \right) =0.\end{equation}
    Then the sequence of the laws of $(\boldsymbol{\Psi}^n)$ is tight in the space of probability measures over $L^2\left([0,T];\mathcal{H}_2\right)$.

