{"artifact_digest":"sha256:04ea72c299186e1ce9102b0646035e435e44ee0eee18423f76b2f95515bf6947","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/04/04ea72c299186e1ce9102b0646035e435e44ee0eee18423f76b2f95515bf6947.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:a8ece1847ed2e86c463af57156ee0c35b7edac559be99f58d543018425383792","bundle_entry_key":"2601-19145-lemma-51-ito-convergence","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_064","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_064.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2601-19145-lem-51-ito-convergence","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: We start by proving the first bullet point. Since each $R_n$ is a contraction in $B$ and $\mathcal{B}$, and $F: B \to \mathcal{B}$ is globally Lipschitz,
\begin{multline*}
    \sup_n\mathbb{E} \int_0^T |\Phi'(x_n(t))F_n(x_n(t))|^{2p}dt \leq \sup_n\mathbb{E} \int_0^T \|\Phi'(x_n(t))\|_{\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{B},\mathbb{R})}^{2p}|F(x_n(t))|_B^{2p}dt \\
    \begin{aligned}
    &\lesssim \sup_n\mathbb{E}\int_0^T \|\Phi'(x_n(t))\|_{\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{B},\mathbb{R})}^{4p} + |x_n(t)|_B^{4p} + |x_0|_B^{4p}dt \\
    &\lesssim \sup_n \|x_n\|_{C([0,T], B)}^{4p} < \infty \,,
\end{aligned}
\end{multline*}
where the last inequalities are from our assumption on $\Phi'$ and the convergence of $x_n$ to $x$ ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:27887f67ba4709478672996bee69eb0f3a5f86708d401eca5d9e07a6fd15ccfa","revision_digest":"sha256:a4314ad1bc9ed1511f0702c9e682454e27c54c77118a864f04220de4197b664b","source_id":"arxiv:2601.19145","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

body:
[Prop hall_projection of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] Let $G$ be a finite bipartite graph with bipartition $(B,W)$. Label every vertex $v\in G$ with a multiplicity $N_v$ such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{v\in W} N_v = \sum_{v\in B} N_v.
    \end{align*}
    There exists an ${\mathbf N}= (N_v)_{v}$-dimer cover of $G$ if and only if there is no set $C\subset W$ such that 
    $$N(C) = \{v\in B : \exists\, c\in C, (c,v)\in E\}$$
    satisfies 
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{v\in N(C)} N_v < \sum_{v\in C} N_v.
    \end{align*}
    We call $U:=C \cup N(C)$ satisfying these conditions an \emph{${\mathbf N}$-counterexample} in $G$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem sig of arXiv:2411.11759, 'Milstein-type schemes for McKean--Vlasov SDEs driven by Brownian motion and Poisson random'] Let Assumptions \mbox{\normalfont  (asum:ic of the paper)}, \mbox{\normalfont  (asum:lip of the paper)} and \mbox{\normalfont  (asump:super of the paper)} to \mbox{\normalfont  (asum:convergence2 of the paper)}  hold 
			with  
   $\bar p\geq \max\{2,\eta/8+1\}(\varepsilon+2)(\eta+1)(\eta+2)/{\varepsilon}\color{black}$ where  $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$.
		 Then,
		\begin{align*}
			&E\big|\sigma\big( x_s^{i, N,n},\mu_s^{x, N,n}\big)-(\widehat{\Lambda})_{tam}^{\displaystyle n}\big(\kappa_{n}(s),s,x_{\kappa_{n}(s)}^{i,N,n},\mu_{\kappa_{n}(s)}^{x,N,n}\big)\big|^2\leq Kn^{-1-\frac{2}{\varepsilon+2}}
		\end{align*}
		for any $s\in[0,T]$ and $i\in\{1,\ldots,N\}$	where $K>0$ is a constant independent of $N$ and $n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 10 (Averaging in invariant H\"older norms) of arXiv:2608.30027, 'Under Ricci flow, a 3-torus goes flat'] Let \(h\) be an invariant metric for the fixed torus
structure, and let \(\mathcal A\) denote the corresponding averaging operator.
For every integer \(k\geq0\), every \(\sigma\in(0,1)\), and every tensor field
\(U\),
\begin{equation}
        \|\mathcal A U\|_{C^{k,\sigma}(M,h)}
        \leq \|U\|_{C^{k,\sigma}(M,h)}.
\end{equation}
Averaging also commutes with constant metric scaling.

[Corollary 9 (Joint distribution of descents, valleys, and cycle type) of arXiv:2606.18047, 'Analysis of the asymmetric shelf shuffle'] For $t\in (0, 1)$, as a formal power series in $u$, one has
\begin{align}
&\frac{t}{1-t}+\sum_{n\ge1}u^n\sum_{\pi\in S_n}\frac{t^{\vl(\pi)+1}}{p^n(1-t)^{n+1}}(p+qt)^{\asc(\pi)-\vl(\pi)}
(q+pt)^{\des(\pi)-\vl(\pi)}X(\pi;\x)

\\
&\qquad=\sum_{m\ge1}t^m\prod_{i\ge1}
\exp\left\{
\sum_{r\ge1}
\frac{x_i^r u^{ir}}{ir}
\sum_{d\mid i}
\mu(d)m^{i/d}
\left(1-\left(-\frac{q}{p}\right)^{dr}\right)^{i/d}
\right\}.
\notag
\end{align}
The same identity holds with \(\vl(\pi)\) replaced by \(\pk(\pi)\).

[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2607.21443, 'The critical KPZ scale for the averaging process'] Let $\lambda=7/8$. Then, we have the following convergence in law
	\begin{equation}
		\ensuremath{\mathcal Y}_{\lambda,N}
		\xLongrightarrow[N\to\infty]{}
		\ensuremath{\mathcal Y}\; \text{,}\;\;
		\qquad
		\text{in}\  \ensuremath{\mathcal D}([0,\infty);\mathscr M_f(\mathbb{R}))\; \text{.}
	\end{equation}
	Here, $\ensuremath{\mathcal Y}=\ensuremath{\mathcal Y}(t,z)$ is the unique continuous It\^o--Walsh solution to the
	multiplicative SHE
	\begin{equation}
		\partial_t\ensuremath{\mathcal Y}(t,z)
		=
		\tfrac12\partial_z^2\ensuremath{\mathcal Y}(t,z)
		+
		{\tfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}}\,\ensuremath{\mathcal Y}(t,z)\,\xi(t,z)\; \text{,}\;\;
		\qquad
		\ensuremath{\mathcal Y}(0,{\,\cdot\,})=\delta_0\; \text{,}\;\;
	\end{equation}
	where $\xi=\xi(t,z)$ is a standard space-time white noise on
	$\mathbb{R}_+\times\mathbb{R}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary 31 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] Let $X$ have density $f$ with respect to $\pi_{T}.$ If $H(X|\pi_{T}) < \infty$ and $t<T,$ then
    \begin{displaymath}
        \Big(  \Psi \big( P_{T-s} f , DP_{T - s} f \big) \Big)_{0 \le s \le t} 
    \end{displaymath}
    is uniformly integrable with respect to the corresponding family of measures $(\pi_{s})_{0 \le s \le t}.$

