  implies that $\iota\bigl( \ball(0,r\mid \YY)\bigr) \subset \XX$ satisfies","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:20eaa79377d1257751b2f5ac4c78b343826835025b158c9cebbadb618ef2b7ab","revision_digest":"sha256:a7f2a1edc4eacb131201d9c70988606daef06b746e3553c8df4545d3947ab977","source_id":"arxiv:2608.30549","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

body:
[Lem l-v-lemma of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] For $V$ as in (equation def-of-V-for-SPDE of the paper) we have $V \in \tilde{\mathcal{D}}^{ext}_+(\mathcal{M}_+)$ (see (spde-tilde-domain of the paper)) and
    \begin{equation*}
    \begin{aligned}
        \mathcal{L} V(r,v,z) &= h'(r)r\Big[\ip{v}{\tilde e_u} + \ip{\tilde F_u(r,v,z)}{1_u}\Big] + \frac{1}{2}h''(r)r^2\|\tilde G_u(r,v,z)^*1_u\|_U^2 \\
        \Gamma V(r,v,z) &= h'(r)^2r^2\|\tilde G_u(r,v,z)^*1_u\|_U^2 \,,
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
where the adjoint of $G_u(r,v,z)$ is taken in $\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{U},L^2)$ and $\tilde e$ is as in (def:tilde-e of the paper).

[Definition of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] Consider a matrix $X\in M^{n\times m}(\mathbb{C})$ with all i.i.d.\ complex Gaussian entries such that
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{E} X_{j,k}=\mathbb{E}X_{j,k}^2=0,~\mathbb{E}|X_{j,k}|^2=1
    \end{equation*}
    for any $j,~k$. Then the Hermitian matrix $W_{n,m}:=X^*X\in M^{m}(\mathbb{C})$ is called a \textbf{complex Wishart matrix}, or a \textbf{Laguerre Ensemble}.

[Theorem 17 (Doob's optional stopping version II, general supermartingale) of arXiv:2606.17452, 'Optional Stopping for Superhedging Supermartingales'] Let $\{f_j\}_{j \geq 0}$ be a given  real-valued supermartingale on $\mathcal{S}$. Consider two stopping times satisfying  
$\rho\le \tau$ a.e. and assume that $(L)-a.e$ holds. Then, if $\tau \leq C ~~a.e.$, where $C$ is a non negative integer:
\begin{equation}
\overline{\sigma}_{\rho} f_{\tau} \leq f_{\rho}\;\;\mbox{a.e. on}~~\mathcal{S}.
\end{equation}

[Theorem 19 of arXiv:2608.19065, 'Boundedly exchangeable graphs'] Let $\xi$ be a boundedly exchangeable graph process on $S$ directed by $\nu$. For every $B \in \widehat{\Bcal}(S)$ where $\pbb(\nu(B) = 0) < 1$ there exists an infinite jointly exchangeable matrix $A^B \in \rbb^{\nbb \times \nbb}$ such that the adjacency matrix $(A, N)$ of any i.i.d.\ representation of $\xi\vert_B$ satisfies for all $n \geq 1$:
    \begin{equation} 
        A \mid (N = n) \overset{d}{=} A^B[n]
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, the distribution of $A^B$ is unique.

[Lemma 19 (Hodge a priori estimates: null-form rhs) of arXiv:2609.01093, 'Ideal MHD below the classical well-posedness threshold'] Let $\Ufield,\Vfield$ be as above, recall $\sigma=s-1>(n-1)/2$, and let
$F[\Ufield]$ be the canonical Hodge representative \eqref{eq:FU}.  Then $F[\Ufield]\in C(I;L^4)$, with
\begin{equation}
 \|F[\Ufield]\|_{L^\infty(I;L^4)}\le C_\delta R^2 ,
\end{equation}
and, for every $\rpar\in[0,1]$ such that
$\Ufield\in H^{\sigma,1+\rpar}_{\breg}$,
\begin{equation}
 \|dF[\Ufield]\|_{L^2(I;Y^{\sigma,\rpar})}
 \le C_{\delta,\rpar} 
 \|\Ufield\|_{H^{\sigma,1}_{\breg}} 
 \|\Ufield\|_{H^{\sigma,1+\rpar}_{\breg}} .
\end{equation}
In particular at $\rpar=0$,
\begin{equation}
 \|dF[\Ufield]\|_{L^2(I;H^\sigma)}\le C_\delta R^2 .
\end{equation}
Finally,
\begin{equation}
 \|dF[\Ufield]-dF[\Vfield]\|_{L^2(I;H^\sigma)}
 \le C_\delta R(1+R) D .
\end{equation}

[Definition 1 (Tempered fractional Brownian motion
\cite{MeerschaertSabzikar2013}) of arXiv:2512.04646, '\textbf{Canonical Rough Path over Tempered Fractional Brownian Motion: Existence, Construc'] For $H \in (0,1)$ and $\lambda > 0$, the \emph{tempered fractional
Brownian motion} (tfBm)
$B_{H,\lambda} = (B_{H,\lambda}(t))_{t \ge 0}$ is the centred
Gaussian process defined by
\begin{equation}

B_{H,\lambda}(t) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(H+\frac{1}{2})} \int_{\mathbb{R}}
\Bigl[ e^{-\lambda(t-s)_+}(t-s)_+^{H-\frac{1}{2}}
      - e^{-\lambda(-s)_+}(-s)_+^{H-\frac{1}{2}} \Bigr] \, dW(s),
\end{equation}
where $W$ is a standard two-sided Brownian motion and
$x_+ = \max(x,0)$.

[Theorem 3 (Reduction; conditional on Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:red}) of arXiv:2608.15987, 'Threshold cascades of interacting diffusions on unimodular random trees: a conditional bra'] Under Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:red}, the failed cluster of the root converges in distribution (as a random rooted tree) to the family tree of the multitype Galton--Watson process on type set $D$ with mean matrix
\begin{equation}
M_{d,d'}=m_*\,q(d')\,p_{d,d'},\qquad d,d'\in D,
\end{equation}
where $p_{d,d'}$ is the transmission probability \eqref{eq:wald}. In particular the survival probability of the cascade is positive iff $\rho(M)>1$, and zero iff $\rho(M)\le1$, where $\rho(M)$ is the Perron root of the nonnegative matrix $M$.

[Lem setup2 of arXiv:2412.09535, 'Local limit theorem for joint subgraph counts'] Let $H\in \mathcal{H}$ be connected and let $\eps \leq \alpha \leq 1$ be real. We get $\abs{\sete[e^{if}]} = n^{-\omega_{\lambda,\ell,\eps}(1)}$ as long as
\begin{cond}
    \item  $n^{-\alpha+\eps} \leq \Delta_H^2 \leq 1.1\pi^2 (p(1-p))^{e(H)}$;
    \item  For any graph $H' \succ H$, we have $n^{v(H')} \Delta_{H'}^2 < n^{v(H)- \alpha - \eps}$.
\end{cond}

[Defi $K$-fuzz structure of arXiv:2607.28817, 'Limiting spectral distribution for the adjacency matrix of the Watts--Strogatz random grap'] We need a few definitions to be able to formally introduce the full $K$-fuzz graph model, denoted $F(K,p)$. See Figure~(fig:fullkfuzz of the paper) for an illustration.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Full $K$-path.} A full $K$-path is the infinite rooted graph with vertex set $\mathbb{Z}$ in which each vertex is adjacent to its $K/2$ nearest neighbors on either side.\\
        \item \textbf{Reduced $K$-path.} A reduced $K$-path is a rooted full $K$-path from which one of the $K/2$ edges connecting the root to its rightmost neighbors chosen uniformly at random is removed.\\
        \item \textbf{Full $K$-fuzz and reduced $K$-fuzz.} A full $K$-fuzz is obtained by starting from a full $K$-path and, for each node, independently rewiring each of its rightmost edges with probability $p$. Each rewired edge becomes a shortcut to a new full $K$-fuzz, producing an independent recursive copy. In addition, each node receives $\poisson(\frac{pK}{2})$ incoming shortcuts, each connecting to the root of a reduced $K$-fuzz. The reduced $K$-fuzz is defined analogously but starts from a reduced $K$-path.
    \end{itemize}

[Lemma spacetimeaverageagain of arXiv:2507.10545, 'KPZ equation from a class of nonlinear SPDEs in infinite volume'] \fsp Fix any {\small$\mathfrak{q}_{\n_{2}}$} in (equation eq:qnformI2 of the paper). We have the following (with notation to be explained afterwards):

\begin{align}
N\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{0}}[\mathscr{S}^{N}\star\mathfrak{q}_{\n_{2}}[\tau_{\cdot}\bphi_{\t}]\mathbf{Z}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}]_{\x}&=\sum_{\substack{{m}=0,\ldots,\mathrm{M}\\0<|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|,\ldots,|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|\leq\mathfrak{n}_{\mathbf{Av}}}}\tfrac{1}{|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|\ldots|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|}\mathrm{b}'_{N,{m},\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{1}}\ldots\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}[\mathscr{S}^{N}\star(\mathbf{Av}^{\mathbf{T},\mathbf{X},\mathfrak{q}_{\n_{2}}}_{\t,\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}\mathbf{S}^{N}_{\t,\cdot})]_{\x}\\
&+\sum_{\substack{{m}=0,\ldots,\mathrm{M}\\0<|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|,\ldots,|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|\leq\mathfrak{n}_{\mathbf{Av}}}}\tfrac{1}{|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|\ldots|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|}\mathrm{b}'_{N,{m},\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}\grad^{\mathbf{T},\mathrm{av}}_{\mathfrak{t}_{\mathbf{Av}}}\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{1}}\ldots\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}[\mathscr{S}^{N}\star(\mathbf{Av}^{\mathbf{T},\mathbf{X},\mathfrak{q}_{\n_{2}}}_{\t,\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}\mathbf{S}^{N}_{\t,\cdot})]_{\x}\\
&+\mathrm{Err}_{2}[\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}\mathbf{S}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}]_{\x}.
\end{align}

\begin{itemize}
\item The parameter {\small$\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{O}(1)$} is a positive integer.
\item The {\small$\mathrm{b}'_{N,{m},\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}$} are deterministic and {\small$\mathrm{O}(N)$}. Moreover, {\small$\mathrm{b}'_{N,{m},\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}=0$} if {\small$\min\{|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|,\ldots,|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|\}\geq2$}.
\item The term {\small$\mathrm{Err}_{2}[\mathbf{R}^{N}\mathbf{S}^{N}]_{\t,\x}$} is a linear operator in {\small$\mathbf{S}^{N}$} evaluated at {\small$(\t,\x)$}, and it satisfies the deterministic estimate below as long as {\small$\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}(1)$} is sufficiently large:

\begin{align}
|\mathrm{Err}_{2}[\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}\mathbf{S}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}]_{\x}|\lesssim N^{-\frac32}\cdot N^{-1+\delta_{\mathbf{S}}}\sum_{|\w|\lesssim N^{1-\delta_{\mathbf{S}}}}\Big(1+\sum_{|\z|\lesssim1}|\bphi_{\t,\x+\w+\z}|^{\mathrm{C}}\Big)\cdot|\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\x+\w}|\cdot|\mathbf{S}^{N}_{\t,\x+\w}|.
\end{align}

\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

