{"artifact_digest":"sha256:caf5f3710c3d2ecf488ac6fd17640f93bff71a8b30baf433276b5086cf57b930","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/ca/caf5f3710c3d2ecf488ac6fd17640f93bff71a8b30baf433276b5086cf57b930.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:43fd6ae781d899a7be9e602b9777bf1a489d60ef0455e89ee84a9a957d3620ea","bundle_entry_key":"2608-07236-theorem-18-negative-association-or-indepe","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_050","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_050.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-07236-proposition-18-negative-association-or-indepe","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: We start by rewriting all of the LPP increments of $h$ in terms of a new environment $h^*$, which is defined below in (equation hstar of the paper).
To simplify the notation, for $m < n - 1$, let 
\eq{ 
\pi^{m,n-1} = \sigma^{n-1} \cdots \sigma^{m+1} \sigma^m \qquad \textup{ and } \qquad \pi_{m,n-1} = \sigma_{m} \cdots \sigma_{n-2} \sigma_{n-1}. 
}
With this notation, define the new environment $h^*$ as 
\begin{equation}
h^* =\pi^{-k, n-1}\cdots \pi^{-2, n-1}\pi^{-1, n-1} h.
\end{equation}
