{"artifact_digest":"sha256:82b607477aa8d1122190c26ed76bfa2cccf1f6c08f46388eca2f68fa057478cf","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/82/82b607477aa8d1122190c26ed76bfa2cccf1f6c08f46388eca2f68fa057478cf.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:2996c9456d45c385a2b63e15997f4a72323882768e8d16196a97a8a721e6101d","bundle_entry_key":"2608-25279-lemma-8-overlap-of-consecutive-interva","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_035","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_035.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-25279-lemma-8-overlap-of-consecutive-interva","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: This sharpens [GoujaudTaylorDieuleveut2025]; we follow their argument, keeping each inequality strict. Write $u=\kappa^{-1}$ and adopt the notation of their Notation~B.1, in normalized variables:
\begin{equation}
 A_m=\beta-\cos\theta_m+u(1-\beta\cos\theta_m),
 \quad
 B_m=\sqrt{A_m^2-2u(1-\cos\theta_m)(1+\beta^2-2\beta\cos\theta_m)},
\end{equation}
so that $s_\pm(\beta,m;\kappa)=A_m\pm B_m$. We first claim the strict form of their inequality~(23),
\begin{equation}
 (A_m-A_{m+1})^2<B_m^2-B_{m+1}^2 .
\end{equation}
