[Theorem of arXiv:2606.01944, 'Propagation of Chaos for Mean-field Mean Reflected Backward Stochastic Differential Equati'] For any $1\leq i\leq N$, set 
\begin{align*}
\hat{Y}^i:=Y^i-\bar{Y}^i, \ \hat{K}:=K^{(N)}-K,\ \hat{Z}^i:=Z^i-\bar{Z}^i.
\end{align*}
Then, there exists a constant $C$ independent of $N$, such that
\begin{align*}
\mathsf{E}\left[\sup_{t\in[0,T]}|\hat{Y}^i_t|^2\right]\leq CN^{-1/2}, \ \mathsf{E}\left[\sup_{t\in[0,T]}|\hat{K}_t|^2\right]\leq CN^{-1/2}, \ \mathsf{E}\left[\int_0^T|\hat{Z}^i_t|^2dt\right]\leq CN^{-1/2}.
\end{align*}

