Let $\mathbb{X}$ and $\mathbb{Y}$ be filtered processes. A coupling $\pi \in \operatorname{Cpl}(\mathbb{X}, \mathbb{Y})$ is called: (i) causal, if for every $t \in \{1, \dots, T-1\}$, $\mathcal{F}^{\mathbb{X}, \mathbb{Y}}_{T, 0}$ and $\mathcal{F}^{\mathbb{X}, \mathbb{Y}}_{0, t}$ are conditionally independent given $\mathcal{F}^{\mathbb{X}, \mathbb{Y}}_{t, 0}$ under $\pi$; (ii) anticausal, if $\mathcal{F}^{\mathbb{X}, \mathbb{Y}}_{0, T}$ and $\mathcal{F}^{\mathbb{X}, \mathbb{Y}}_{t, 0}$ are conditionally independent given $\mathcal{F}^{\mathbb{X}, \mathbb{Y}}_{0, t}$ under $\pi$; and (iii) bicausal, if it is both causal and anticausal. The set of all bicausal couplings is denoted by $\operatorname{Cpl}_{\mathrm{bc}}(\mathbb{X}, \mathbb{Y})$.
\mathbb{X}, \mathbb{Y} are filtered processes
