[Lemma deteministic of arXiv:2411.04590, 'Singular stochastic delay equations driven by fractional Brownian motion: Dynamics, longti'] Consider the equation (equation linear delay of the paper) and recall the definition of $\Delta$ from (equation characteristic of the paper). Set
			\begin{align}
				\lambda_1 = \max \{ \operatorname{Re}(z) \mid \det(\Delta(z)) = 0 \}.
			\end{align}
			Then, for every $\xi \in C([-r,0], \mathbb{R}^n)$, we have:
			\begin{align}
				\limsup_{t \to \infty} \frac{1}{t} \log \| T_{t} \xi \|_{C^1([-r,0], \mathbb{R}^n)} \leq \lambda_1.
			\end{align}
			Here, \((T_t)_{t \geq 0}\) denotes the semigroup solution as defined in (equation delay-semigroup of the paper), and \(\|\cdot\|_{C^1([-r,0], \mathbb{R}^n)}\) is the usual \(C^1([-r,0], \mathbb{R}^n)\) norm, i.e., the supremum norm of the path and its derivative. Also 
			\begin{align}
				\lim_{t \geq r, t\rightarrow\infty}\frac{1}{t}\log\sup_{\|\xi\|_{\infty,[-r,0]}=1}\log \| T_{t} \xi \|_{C^1([-r,0], \mathbb{R}^n)} = \lambda_{1}.
			\end{align}

